WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.075
Modified endowment contracts and the seven-pay test.

2
00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:08.359
What happens when a life insurance policy is funded too quickly.

3
00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:13.614
Federal tax law limits how fast a life insurance policy can be funded.

4
00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:15.518
It's called the seven-pay test.

5
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.782
During the first seven years,

6
00:00:17.841 --> 00:00:22.616
the total premiums paid can't exceed what would have been paid by that point if the

7
00:00:22.636 --> 00:00:25.561
policy were paid up with seven level annual premiums.

8
00:00:26.107 --> 00:00:31.145
Stay under that limit, and the policy is taxed as normal life insurance.

9
00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:34.355
But pay in too much, too soon,

10
00:00:34.414 --> 00:00:38.720
and the policy becomes a modified endowment contract, or MEC.

11
00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:43.806
And once a policy is a MEC, it stays a MEC permanently.

12
00:00:44.741 --> 00:00:45.994
So what changes?

13
00:00:46.053 --> 00:00:47.503
Some things stay the same.

14
00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:51.311
The death benefit is still generally income-tax free,

15
00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:54.121
and the cash value still grows tax-deferred.

16
00:00:55.085 --> 00:00:58.626
What changes is money taken out while the insured is living.

17
00:00:59.172 --> 00:01:03.223
Loans and withdrawals come out gains first, which is called last in, first out,

18
00:01:03.247 --> 00:01:05.487
and they're taxed as ordinary income.

19
00:01:05.941 --> 00:01:11.548
Before age fifty-nine and a half, there's generally a ten percent penalty on top.

20
00:01:12.488 --> 00:01:13.602
Here's an example.

21
00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:17.131
A forty-five-year-old has fifteen thousand dollars of gain in a MEC,

22
00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.314
and takes a ten-thousand-dollar loan.

23
00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:24.701
Because gains come out first, the full ten thousand is taxable as ordinary income,

24
00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:27.534
plus a ten percent penalty of one thousand dollars.

25
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:32.263
The same loan from a policy that isn't a MEC wouldn't be taxed while the policy

26
00:01:32.283 --> 00:01:33.350
stays in force.

27
00:01:33.804 --> 00:01:38.830
And an exam clue: single premium whole life is usually a MEC.
